@page rh="HOUSE AUREM · ABIGAIL · FAITH AND SCRIPTURE" m="1 SAM 25; 1 SAM 25.24–31; VOL. I · {v1:hannah} · {v1:cast}"
# Faith and scripture
rec:
What she believes | OPEN QUESTION: Nothing on file. INFERENCE: A woman who reads people for a living believes in what works in a room.
What the Temple teaches about a woman like her | POSSIBLE: The Temple holds up the biblical Abigail as the model wife who calms an angry man with gifts and humility, and drops the part where she acts without telling her husband.
Her name | Abigail, Nabal's wife, who loads donkeys with food, rides out without telling her husband, kneels, takes the blame on herself, and talks an armed David out of a massacre he'd regret as king (1 Sam 25). The Talmud counts her among seven women prophets (Vol. I, {v1:hannah}).
Where it breaks | POSSIBLE: The biblical Abigail persuades a powerful man on her knees, once, on a road. This one teaches a girl how to do it on camera, every week, for a House.
tab:
| She'd quote | Never read to her |
| 1 Samuel 25.24, "Upon me, my lord, upon me let this iniquity be" | 1 Samuel 25.19, "But she told not her husband Nabal" |
small: Volume One: Hannah, Abigail and Michal, {v1:hannah}. The names in the cast, {v1:cast}.
prompt: What Abigail says to Eden that the biblical Abigail said to David | fill

